3.2. Two-dimensional problem
Lifting line theory requires an analysis of the unsteady aerodynamics of a two-dimensional airfoil. For helicopter applications, two main classes of methods are used to tackle this problem, Theodorsen’s theory [17] and the indicial method as developed by Beddoes [18]. Both have the advantage to allow to use the experimental 2D airfoil data in order to include compressibility and viscosity effects. However, the latter method, based on the decomposition of the unsteady time-varying motion of the airfoil into elementary step functions, computes the corresponding airfoil load by linear superposition of the elementary indicial responses using Duhamel’s principle. This makes it necessary to model the 2D airfoil polar instead of directly using experimental data, and the unsteady response is parameterised too by adjusting the loads to experimental or CFD data, thus allow
